# The Upanishads and Vedanta: Brahman, Atman, and Liberation

> The Upanishads turn from ritual to the self, teaching that the inner
> Atman and the cosmic Brahman are one. Vedanta built centuries of
> rival systems on that single identity claim.

## 1. What the Upanishads Are

The **Upanishads** are the speculative culmination of the Vedas —
hence **Vedanta**, "the end of the Vedas", meaning both their closing
portions and their goal. Tradition counts **108** Upanishads (the
Muktika list), of which about **thirteen principal** ones are
philosophically central, composed roughly between 800 and 200 BCE.

They are **shruti** — "heard" revelation — transmitted in dialogues:
Yajnavalkya debating at King Janaka's court, or teaching his wife
Maitreyi that wealth serves no purpose without immortality.

[definition]
**Brahman** is the sole ultimate reality behind all appearance,
described as **sat-chit-ananda** — being, consciousness, and bliss.
**Atman** is the true self, the innermost witness, not identical with
body or mind. The Upanishadic thesis is that Atman *is* Brahman.
[/definition]

## 2. The Great Sentences (Mahavakyas)

Each Veda's Upanishad contributes a "great sentence" compressing the
identity claim:

| Mahavakya | Meaning | Upanishad |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Tat tvam asi | That thou art | Chandogya |
| Aham brahmasmi | I am Brahman | Brihadaranyaka |
| Prajnanam brahma | Consciousness is Brahman | Aitareya |
| Ayam atma brahma | This self is Brahman | Mandukya |

## 3. Ignorance, Illusion, Liberation

If the self is already Brahman, why the search? Because **avidya**
(individual ignorance) and **maya** (the cosmic power of appearance)
conceal the identity, projecting the many on the one. **Moksha** —
liberation from rebirth — comes not by better ritual but by **jnana**,
the direct knowledge "I am Brahman", which dissolves the ignorance.

[important]
This is the great Upanishadic pivot: knowledge replaces sacrifice.
Where the Vedic Samhitas and Brahmanas command doing, the Upanishads
insist on knowing.
[/important]

## 4. Advaita: Shankara

The line runs from **Gaudapada** — whose Mandukya Karika argues
**ajativada**, that nothing ever truly originates — to his disciple's
disciple **Adi Shankara** (traditional dates 788–820 CE), the giant of
**Advaita Vedanta**, "non-dualism".

- **Brahma satyam** — Brahman alone is real.
- **Jagat mithya** — the world is appearance: neither real as Brahman,
  nor utterly non-existent like a hare's horn.
- **Jivo brahmaiva naparah** — the soul is none other than Brahman.

Shankara wrote commentaries (**bhasyas**) on the whole Prasthanatrayi —
the Upanishads, the Brahma Sutras, and the Bhagavad Gita — and
distinguished two levels of truth: the practical (vyavaharika) and
the ultimate (paramarthika).

## 5. Vishishtadvaita: Ramanuja

**Ramanuja** (1017–1137) founded **Vishishtadvaita**, "qualified
non-dualism", for the Sri Vaishnava tradition:

- One Brahman, but **with attributes (saguna)** — personal, qualified.
- Souls and matter are real, as the body and modes of Brahman: utterly
  dependent, yet genuinely distinct.
- He rejected Shankara's **maya** as disguised Buddhism, reading the
  Upanishads theistically.

Liberation comes through **bhakti** (devotion) and **prapatti** —
complete self-surrender to God's grace — not knowledge alone. His
Sribhashya on the Brahma Sutras is the classic statement.

## 6. Dvaita: Madhva

**Madhva** (1238–1317) taught **Dvaita**, uncompromising dualism:
God, souls, and matter are eternally, irreducibly different. His
**five eternal differences (pancha-bheda)**:

1. God differs from the soul.
2. God differs from matter.
3. Souls differ from one another.
4. Souls differ from matter.
5. One piece of matter differs from another.

Vishnu is supreme; the world is real; liberation is attained through
devotion, and souls differ even in their final capacities.

@diagram tree id="philosophy-vedanta-tree" layout="tree" aria="Vedanta schools from Advaita to Dvaita with founders"
  Vedanta -> Advaita
  Vedanta -> Vishishtadvaita
  Vedanta -> Dvaita
  Vedanta -> Dvaitadvaita
  Vedanta -> Shuddhadvaita
  Advaita -> Shankara
  Vishishtadvaita -> Ramanuja
  Dvaita -> Madhva
@/diagram

## 7. Comparing the Three Great Vedantas

| Issue | Advaita (Shankara) | Vishishtadvaita (Ramanuja) | Dvaita (Madhva) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| World | Appearance (mithya) | Real, Brahman's body | Real and independent in kind |
| Self | Brahman itself | Part / mode of Brahman | Eternally distinct |
| God | Nirguna, no attributes | Saguna, with attributes | Vishnu, supreme and personal |
| Path | Discriminating knowledge (jnana) | Devotion (bhakti), surrender (prapatti) | Devotion and God's grace |
| Reality count | One: Brahman | One with real modes | Three eternally distinct orders |

Other subschools in one line: **Nimbarka's dvaitadvaita**
(difference-and-non-difference) and **Vallabha's shuddhadvaita** (pure
non-dualism through grace).

## 8. The Long Echo

Vedanta read the Bhagavad Gita as its synoptic text, fed the Bhakti
movement's poetry, and travelled abroad when **Vivekananda** addressed
the World's Parliament of Religions at **Chicago in 1893**, announcing
the harmony of religions and founding the Ramakrishna Mission — the
start of modern Neo-Vedanta.

[summary]
The Upanishads teach that Atman is Brahman — sat-chit-ananda — hidden
by maya and avidya, unveiled by knowledge. Shankara's Advaita declares
the world appearance and the soul Brahman; Ramanuja's Vishishtadvaita
keeps souls real as God's body, saved by surrender; Madhva's Dvaita
posits five eternal differences. From Badarayana's Brahma Sutras to
Vivekananda in 1893, it is one continuous argument.
[/summary]
